-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.18 (2026-10-31)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * The C and C++ migration is paid once (ORDER 315, from what ORDER 310
    measured with 0.1.17). restore() tests the names it is given as a set:
    with the list the migration passed, its filter took 68.9 s on the
    station's 90,217 records and 47,360 C and C++ names (55.1 s on a
    synthetic install of that size; 0.55 s now).
  * A C or C++ file whose package version neither APT's cache nor its
    lists hold waits: its version is remembered with the lists and the
    cache as they were when it was tried, and it is tried again only when
    its package, the lists or the cache change. With 38 such versions
    (13,239 files, the station's), every dpkg run read all records and made
    38 failing downloads, about 80 s a run on the station (2.4 s and 38
    attempts a run on the synthetic install); now a dpkg run reads one
    small table (0.001 s, no attempt). One apt-cache madison call says
    which versions the lists still offer, so no download is tried for one
    they lack. --repair-consumers tries them all now; --verify names them
    (PENDING), and they are not problems: their line numbers are the
    authored ones.
  * Progress lines on the terminal apt shows: before the long restore and
    projection, and every 1,000 files; standard output is line-buffered.
  * dpkg's database is read with its journal (/var/lib/dpkg/updates), as
    dpkg reads it. Inside a dpkg run the status file alone is out of date:
    during a first install it listed 81 packages as installed where dpkg's
    database (dpkg-query) listed 154, and the postinst recorded those 81,
    without libstdc++-16-dev. The hooks are not active in the run
    that installs this package (their file is a conffile, in place only at
    its configure), so a trigger (interest-noawait) projects what the run
    configures after it, at the end of the run.
  * A migration inside a dpkg hook leaves packages.tsv as it was, so the
    packages that same run changed are still projected by post-invoke
    (0.1.17 marked them all as seen when it projected again).
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.17 (2026-10-31)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * C and C++ keep each removed comment's line breaks instead of writing #line
    after every removed run: every authored line keeps its number, and GCC no
    longer pays for the directives (ORDER 303; with #line, preprocessing
    Debian's libstdc++ headers took 24.8% more instructions than the
    originals). The pointer takes a line a removed comment emptied.
  * The GCC check also compares where every logical line starts, the lines
    of every fully preprocessed token, and refuses any added line control.
  * On upgrade, --repair-consumers and the dpkg hooks project again the C and
    C++ files an older renderer projected with #line: exact originals first,
    files the person changed left as they are; a file whose original is
    missing keeps its old form and is the only one tried again.
  * --resolve maps C and C++ columns through the renderer's map.
  * The renderer identity names the form and carries its code's digest
    (lean 0.1.17); it changes whenever the renderer's code does.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.16 (2026-10-20)
  * Locate the actual Source stanza when adding build dependencies. Preserve
    leading licence-comment paragraphs, as used by Abseil's Debian packaging.
    Regression checks use Debian's parser. Projection semantics are unchanged.

  Version 0.1.11 (2026-10-20)
  * Retain dependency-field comments and continuation separators when adding
    build prerequisites; checked with Debian's parser against the QEMU/digiKam
    failure pattern. Source and payload projection semantics are unchanged.

  Version 0.1.10 (2026-10-20)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * Support explicit C/Java source-language plans in retained build recipes
  * Reject changed language plans and failed Java qualification before compilation
  * Preserve GLSL shaders: GCC is not their preprocessor, and shader line-number
    semantics need their own qualified adapter
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.9 (2026-10-20)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * Keep structured C/C++ documentation and translation inputs unchanged until
    their generators are qualified, including the original declaration layout
  * Retain reviewed source-consumer preservation plans in native source packages
  * Reject unmatched source patterns and changed preservation plans
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.8 (2026-10-20)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * Keep Debian epochs in package identity and omit them from archive filenames
  * Preserve service symlinks and stable permissions in script source rebuilds
  * Bundle checked adapters for conventional dh and authored script/data packages
  * Qualify empty and comment-only shell programs without changing their runtime
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.7 (2026-10-19)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * Add compiler-backed Java and Kotlin adapters with source location recovery
  * Preserve documentation comments, Kotlin separators and source timestamps
  * Stabilize C validation filenames without hiding real line-number differences
  * Supply native source projection and Debian payload assembly helpers
  * Publish self-contained source beside development channel binaries
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.6 (2026-10-18, staged)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * Recognize lc1 build-time manifests and skip client rendering of marked packages
  * Keep build-time conffile baselines separate from reversible local projections
  * Verify build-time payloads, fetch exact HR companions and resolve their locations
  * Build marked packages before checksums; require payload and HR proof at publication
  * Verify pre-squashfs projections and bind release ISO evidence to final artifact hashes
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.5 (2026-10-17)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * A dpkg started while another one runs calls pre-invoke, stops at dpkg's
    lock and never calls post-invoke; its pre-invoke replaced the list of
    restored conffiles with an empty one, so the running dpkg's post-invoke
    left them in their package's bytes: the list is now added to, never replaced
  * Every dpkg run projects again any recorded conffile found in its package's
    bytes, whatever list it is on; --project does the same for any file
  * verify says RESTORED for a file in its package's own bytes (not projected,
    not a problem), never "changed by the person"
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.4 (2026-10-17)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * An upgrade of dpkg-stereo no longer projects every installed package again:
    the first projection runs on a first install, or after a removal
  * A file that comes back in bytes already projected and checked (a reinstall,
    or a file an upgrade did not change) is rendered again without a second check
  * A package dpkg rewrote in the same second its record was written is looked
    at again by the next dpkg run
  * --restore and --verify --deep read each package's .deb once, not once per file
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.3 (2026-10-17)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * Records kept per package: a dpkg run reads and writes only what it touched
  * One guarded bash per projecting process for the shell checks
  * A few files are projected in-process, without a worker pool
  * Records of 0.1.1 and 0.1.2 are moved over at the first run
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.2 (2026-10-16)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * One acorn process per projecting process: QML and JavaScript checks 70x faster
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.1 (2026-10-16)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * MIT licence (Daniel's decision)
  * After dpkg, only packages it changed and conffiles restored for it are read
  * Files changed since their package installed them are left alone
  * Slimmer records; the build's reasons stay out of the image
  * verify: package versions, pristine conffiles, --deep against the .debs
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.0 (2026-10-16)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * First version: projection after dpkg, pristine conffiles before dpkg
  * Verify, restore, hold, HR copies of settings, the MR line resolver
  * The pre-squashfs hook of Sparky Backup Core Stereo for the ISO
